g 1.5  celsius and only 2.7 degree rises in  temperatures this century in practical  terms what would that mean what would it  look like  it's a world we really don't want to  find out about  large areas of the earth would have  unlivable heat  at  parts of the year um we would see the  kind of climb impacts we saw last summer  of massive heat waves in canada  of floods washing houses into  the  rivers in germany and people up to their  necks in  water on the subway system in china  happening with great frequency and  for me i think it's very hard to see how  agriculture globally can adapt to that  kind of level  of change uh and keep food supplies  going for what will at that point be uh  eight or nine billion people on earth it  would be a real catastrophe and world  leaders um  in in these next two weeks really need  to um  push hard to have practical policies to  reduce emissions in the short term and  simon we heard that warning from the  prime minister yesterday that that he's  really worried we've had saudi arabia's  pledges we've had australia today but  the problem has been highlighted that  many of those net zero goals remain  vague we haven't got the details of them  and much of the heavy lifting is pushed  to after  2030  is anything in glasgow going to change  that  well i do think that these net zero  pledges are important because they bring  climate action  two decades earlier and that means that  we then start talking about what is  going to happen in the 2020s and what to  watch out for at this summit is the  first two days when more than 120 world  leaders will be in glasgow making  pledges and making announcements and  that's the time to look for potentially  big announcements about what might  happen over the next decade  you
 

